# MobX security

Stack-specific risks narrowing the common OWASP-style rules to MobX state
management. Most web security concerns (XSS, auth, injection) are
React/general-web concerns already covered by the common and React rules
this file `extends`; this file covers the risks that are specific to
putting sensitive data in observable state.

## Do not put secrets in long-lived observable state

- A token, API key, or credential held in an `@observable` field on a
  store that survives the session (not cleared on logout, persisted via
  `autorun`/`reaction` to `localStorage`) is exposed to any code with
  access to that store instance and to any DevTools-based MobX inspector
  attached to the page. Keep secrets out of observable state; pass them
  through a request layer that does not put them on a reactive object a
  component tree (and any debugging tool observing it) can read.
- If a `reaction`/`autorun` persists store state to `localStorage`/
  `sessionStorage` (a common MobX pattern for "remember this UI state"),
  explicitly exclude any field carrying a token, PII, or payment data from
  what gets serialized — persist a derived, scrubbed snapshot, not the
  whole store.

## Untrusted data into observable state is still untrusted

- Assigning server or user-supplied HTML/markdown into an observable
  field does not sanitize it. If that field is later rendered with
  `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` in an `observer` component, the same XSS risk
  applies as any other React render path — sanitize before it reaches the
  observable, not by trusting MobX's reactivity to be a security boundary
  (see `rules/security.mdc` in the `react` pack this one extends).

## Reaction-driven side effects and SSRF/open-redirect risk

- A `reaction`/`autorun` that fires a `fetch`/navigation based on an
  observable URL or hostname field must validate that value the same way
  a request-time input would be validated — an attacker who can influence
  the observable (e.g. via a query param synced into the store) can
  otherwise trigger a request to an attacker-chosen host purely through
  state changes, with no obvious call site to audit.

## Disposal prevents stale-store data leaks across users/sessions

- A store not disposed on logout/route-change can keep the previous
  user's data in memory and observable to any component that still holds
  a reference (e.g. a lingering `reaction` on a background tab). Call
  `dispose()` on session/user change for any store holding
  user-scoped data, not just to free memory — to stop rendering or
  syncing stale, now-unauthorized data.
